  "rulesTitle": "Rules summary",
  "rulesMenuHint": "The most important scoring rules, by expansion.",
  "rulesBase": "• City: a completed one scores 2 pts per tile and per pennant; an unfinished one (at the end of the game) 1 pt per tile and pennant.\n• Road: 1 pt per tile, completed or not.\n• Cloister: completed 9 pts (the cloister and the 8 surrounding tiles); unfinished 1 pt per tile, including the cloister tile.\n• Farms: at the end of the game 3 pts for each completed city adjacent to the farm.\n• The player with the most meeples in a feature or on a farm scores; on a tie every tied player scores the full points.",
  "rulesInns": "• Cathedral: a completed city with a cathedral scores 3 pts per tile and pennant; an unfinished one 0 pts.\n• Inn: a completed road with an inn scores 2 pts per tile; an unfinished one 0 pts.\n• A big meeple counts as two meeples when determining the majority, but does not double the points.",
  "rulesTraders": "• Goods: for a completed city with a symbol, the player who completed it receives a wine, grain or cloth token.\n• At the end of the game, for each kind of goods the player with the most tokens scores 10 pts (on a tie every tied player scores 10 pts).\n• Pig: a completed city adjacent to a farm with a pig scores 4 pts instead of 3.\n• When determining the majority in a feature or on a farm, the pig and the builder pieces are not counted.",
  "rulesPrincess": "• Fairy: at the start of the turn the player scores 1 pt if their meeple stands next to the fairy.\n• When a feature with a meeple next to the fairy is scored, its owner scores an extra 3 pts, also at the end of the game.\n• The dragon and the princess do not change scoring.",
  "rulesTower": "• The ransom for a captured meeple (also a big one) is 3 pts, received by the player holding the prisoner.\n• A meeple, a big meeple, a wagon and a mayor can be ransomed; builders and pigs cannot be captured.\n• A ransom is paid on the player's own turn, once per turn, and requires at least 3 pts.\n• An exchange of prisoners (two players hold each other's meeples) takes place without points.",
  "rulesAbbey": "• The abbey scores like a cloister: completed 9 pts, unfinished 1 pt per tile.\n• A farm not joined to a barn: normal scoring, 3 pts per completed city.\n• A farm joined to a barn: farmers score 1 pt per city (2 with a pig) and 2 pts per castle.\n• The barn owner at the end of the game: 4 pts per city and 5 pts per castle, no pig bonus.",
  "rulesBridges": "• Castle: its owner scores the points of the completed city, road or cloister it stands next to. A castle does not score at the end of the game.\n• When a farm is scored, a castle is worth 4 pts instead of 3 (5 with a pig).\n• Bazaar: the player who picked the tile buys it from the highest bidder (pays them the price) or sells it to them (receives the price). If only that player bid, they pay a price that nobody receives.\n• Bridges do not change scoring.",
  "rulesHills": "• Vineyards: for a completed cloister its owner scores an extra 3 pts for each vineyard on the surrounding tiles.\n• Sheep: a flock consists of 1–4 sheep tokens; every shepherd on the farm scores for the whole flock. A wolf scatters the flock. Sheep do not score at the end of the game.",
  "rulesCircus": "• Circus: after an animal token is revealed, every meeple on the circus tile and the eight neighboring tiles scores its owner the value of the animal (elephant 7, lion 6, bear 5, seal 4, monkey 3, flea 1). The meeples stay on the board.\n• Acrobats: a completed pyramid (3 meeples) scores 5 pts per acrobat, and the acrobats return to the supply. At the end of the game 5 pts for each acrobat on the board.\n• Ringmaster: 2 pts for each circus or acrobat tile next to him, also at the end of the game.",
  "rulesGold": "• Gold: there are 16 bars. For a completed feature the bars on its tiles go to the player with the majority (not to farmers).\n• At the end of the game the value of a bar depends on how many a player holds: 1–3 bars 1 pt each, 4–6 2 pts each, 7–9 3 pts each, 10+ 4 pts each.",
  "gameStartedTitle": "The game has started!",
  "noPlayersHint2": "To edit a player, press and hold their row.",
  "summaryTitle": "Summary",
  "modifyExpansions": "Modify expansions",
  "backToEdit": "Back to editing players",
  "traderCompleted": "Who completed the city (gets the goods)?",
  "expMonasteries": "German and Dutch monasteries",
  "expMonasteriesDesc": "Monasteries with an abbot who stays until the end of the game and scores along lines: 1 pt for each tile in the monastery row and column plus 1 pt for the monastery itself. A monastery with a monk scores like a normal cloister.",
  "scMonastery": "Abbot",
  "monasteryPointsLabel": "Abbot end-game points",
  "monasteryHint": "Enter the abbot end-game points: 1 pt for each tile in the monastery row and column (counted from the monastery up to the first gap in each direction) and 1 pt for the monastery tile itself. A monastery with a monk scores like a normal cloister – choose \"Cloister\" then.",
  "monasteryEventLabel": "{name}: abbot +{points}",
  "rulesMonasteries": "• The monasteries from the expansion replace the base-game cloisters; the new tiles are shuffled into the stack.\n• After placing the tile a player may deploy a meeple as a monk (scores like a normal cloister, 9 pts when completed) or as an abbot (laid on its side).\n• The abbot stays on the tile until the end of the game and only then scores: 1 pt for each tile in the monastery's row and column plus 1 pt for the monastery itself.\n• An empty space stops the count in that direction.",
  "menuTutorial": "Tutorial",
